    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n64342" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λώπ-η</span></b> , ἡ, (<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λέπω</span>) <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>covering, robe, mantle</i>, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δίπτυχον ἀμφ᾽ ὤμοισιν ἔχουσ᾽ εὐεργέα
					λώπην</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Od.
					13.224
				</span></i>
			, cf. <i><span class="en">
				Theoc.
				25.254
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				A.R.
				2.32
			</span></i>:—also <b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λῶπος</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">εος</span>, τό, <i><span class="en">
				Alc.
				<u>Supp.</u>
				18.2
			</span></i> (dub.), <i><span class="en">
				Hippon.
				3
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Anacr.
				80
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Herod.
				8.36
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Theoc.
				14.66
			</span></i>, Ps.-<i><span class="en">
				Luc.
				<u>Philopatr.</u>
				22
			</span></i>.—Only poetic in class. writers, though prose
			writers have the derivs. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λώπιον,
			λωποδύτης</span>.</div>
